---
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocsRelated from "../../components/docs/DocsRelated.astro";

/* The provider/harness/account axes, which are three different questions the UI asks in one picker and which nothing outside the code explained. */

const providers = [
    {
        name: `Claude Code`,
        connect: `A Claude subscription`,
        note: `The sandbox owns the credential: you sign in to Anthropic, not to intentic.`,
    },
    { name: `Codex`, connect: `A ChatGPT subscription`, note: `Runs on your ChatGPT plan through the bundled translator.` },
    { name: `Grok`, connect: `A SuperGrok subscription`, note: `Signs in with xAI's device-code flow; there is no key to paste.` },
    { name: `Kimi Code`, connect: `A Kimi Code subscription`, note: `Served under Claude Code by default, and under Codex, OpenCode or Kimi's own CLI where you pick them.` },
    {
        name: `Google`,
        connect: `A Google sign-in: no subscription`,
        note: `Labelled for the account rather than the model family: this one channel vends Gemini's models alongside Claude and GPT-OSS. The useful answer for anyone who has connected nothing yet.`,
    },
    {
        name: `Z.ai`,
        connect: `A GLM Coding Plan`,
        note: `Sign in and the sandbox mints the plan's key itself. Say which plan you hold first — international, or BigModel for a mainland one — because a key made for one is refused by the other. Pointed straight at Z.ai's own Anthropic endpoint: nothing translates it, so a GLM turn is an ordinary Claude Code turn against a different host.`,
    },
    {
        name: `Meta`,
        connect: `A Muse Code subscription`,
        note: `Sign in with a code on Meta's page and the sandbox mints the key. Muse Spark, in Meta's own Muse Code agent by default, which the sandbox installs when you connect.`,
    },
    {
        name: "Cursor",
        connect: "A Cursor Pro subscription",
        note: "Runs Cursor's own agent runtime and model catalog through the SDK installed into your sandbox after you connect it.",
    },
    {
        name: "Devin",
        connect: "A Devin account, free or paid",
        note: "Runs Devin's own agent through its CLI, which the sandbox installs when you connect. You sign in on Devin's page, and the sign-in finishes on its own wherever Intentic runs on your computer or browser; elsewhere you paste back what the page gives you. The picker lists the models your Devin plan offers: SWE, Claude, GPT, Gemini and the rest on a paid plan, fewer on the free one.",
    },
];

// One row per provider, the loops it runs on today, default first: the provider table's `loops` in words.
const loops: { provider: string; loops: string; note?: string }[] = [
    { provider: `Claude Code`, loops: `Claude Code.` },
    {
        provider: `Codex`,
        loops: `Codex (its app-server), Claude Code, OpenCode.`,
        note: `Claude Code is outside the apps OpenAI lists for a ChatGPT plan. OpenCode is listed, but through its own sign-in; here it reaches the plan through the translator.`,
    },
    {
        provider: `Grok`,
        loops: `OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, Grok Build.`,
        note: `Claude Code and Codex need a second sign-in of their own, since OpenCode holds the first, and are outside the apps xAI lists for SuperGrok. Grok Build is xAI's own CLI, with its own device sign-in.`,
    },
    {
        provider: `Kimi Code`,
        loops: `Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Kimi CLI.`,
        note: `Kimi documents Codex and OpenCode with a Kimi Code API key; here they reach your plan through the translator's sign-in instead. Kimi CLI is Kimi's own client, with its own device sign-in.`,
    },
    { provider: `Google`, loops: `OpenCode.`, note: `Google refuses any request announcing Claude Code, and its terms don't allow OpenCode either.` },
    { provider: `Cursor`, loops: `Cursor's own agent, through its SDK.` },
    { provider: `Devin`, loops: `Devin's own agent, through its CLI.` },
    {
        provider: `Meta`,
        loops: `Muse Code, Claude Code.`,
        note: `Muse Code is the agent Meta's subscription is for. Claude Code stays on offer, outside Meta's terms, which keep a Muse Code key for Muse Code.`,
    },
    {
        provider: `Z.ai`,
        loops: `Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode.`,
        note: `Each on the plan's own key, aimed at Z.ai's own endpoint for that loop (Anthropic Messages, Responses, Chat Completions), as the GLM Coding Plan documents.`,
    },
    {
        provider: `A model endpoint`,
        loops: `Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, Muse Code.`,
        note: `OpenCode calls the endpoint itself in its own protocol. Codex and Muse Code speak OpenAI's Responses API, which the translator serves for an OpenAI-compatible endpoint; an Anthropic one can't run on either. The free trial runs on Claude Code only.`,
    },
    { provider: `Pi`, loops: `Pi's own loop.` },
    { provider: `An ACP agent`, loops: `The agent's own loop.` },
];

const others = [
    {
        name: `A local model`,
        detail: `The sandbox downloads the weights at a pinned revision, checks them against the published checksum, and serves them itself: pick a model on the Local model card and it appears in the picker. Nothing to install, nothing leaves the machine. It runs on the sandbox's CPU, and the connect page offers the largest model that fits in the memory free right now. For a GPU, run Ollama or LM Studio on the computer that hosts the sandbox and add it as a model endpoint (below). Small models shine on the one-shot jobs (free commit messages, free session titles) and on work that must stay local. The smallest, the one ready in a minute, is kept for those jobs and is not offered for chat.`,
    },
    {
        name: `A model endpoint`,
        detail: `Point the sandbox at any OpenAI-compatible API you already run: Ollama or LM Studio on the computer that hosts the sandbox (the Local models panel finds them by itself), vLLM on the GPU box, a gateway. A server on the hosting computer counts as local, so the privacy shield leaves its traffic alone. Its catalog is read from the server itself, so the picker offers what that server actually serves. A model whose server says its chat template cannot call tools (llama.cpp reports this) is kept for one-shot jobs and is not offered for chat, because an agent turn is made of tool calls.`,
    },
    { name: `An ACP agent`, detail: `Any agent speaking the Agent Client Protocol, added as a capability. Adding one needs no update to intentic.` },
];
---

<DocsLayout
    pageId="models"
    heading="Models & accounts"
    lead="Which model serves a turn is four questions, not one: the provider, the account, the agentic loop it runs in, and the model itself. The picker asks all four; this is what each of them changes."
>
    <h2>Whose credentials these are</h2>
    <p>
        Every credential here belongs to your sandbox. You connect your own subscription and turns spend{" "}
        <em>your</em> allowance. intentic never resells inference, and the platform never holds the token.
    </p>

    <h2>Providers</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Provider</th><th>What you connect</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                providers.map((provider) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>{provider.name}</strong></td>
                        <td>
                            {provider.connect}. <span class="text-subtle">{provider.note}</span>
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Two more sources are open-ended rather than built in:</p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Also</th><th>What it is</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                others.map((other) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>{other.name}</strong></td>
                        <td>{other.detail}</td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Connect them on <strong>Sandbox → Agent</strong>. A subscription connects by signing in; nothing asks you for an API key you'd have to rotate by hand.</p>

    <h2>Several accounts of one provider</h2>
    <p>
        A sandbox can hold more than one account per provider: a work Claude and a personal one, two ChatGPT
        seats, and a turn can name which serves it. Absent, it takes the provider's first.
    </p>
    <p>
        Naming one matters most where nobody is watching. When an automation's first account runs out of headroom, or belongs to an organisation that switched the plan off, every unattended run errors against it until somebody reads the row. That is why an automation can pin an account and a chat rarely bothers.
    </p>
    <p>
        An account belongs to a provider, so an automation can only pin one while every model on its ladder is on that same provider. Spread the ladder across providers and the pin goes: the daemon then serves each fire from whichever connected account has the most headroom, which is what you wanted from the pin anyway.
    </p>

    <h2>What an automation runs on</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>An automation names its own models, in order, and it cannot be saved without them.</strong> It is the one thing here that
        spends an allowance with nobody in the room, on a schedule you set once and rarely re-read — so nothing is chosen for it. There is no
        sandbox-wide tier behind an automation and no inheriting whatever your chat happens to be set to this week.
    </p>
    <p>
        The list is a ladder for the same reason every other model list in this product is one, only more so: a chat refuses in front of
        somebody who can retry it, while a wake at 3am against a spent allowance simply does not happen and nobody finds out until morning.
        Each rung carries its own model, reasoning tier, speed and harness; the daemon walks them at fire time and takes the first one this
        sandbox can actually start. An automation whose every provider has since been disconnected records the fire as an error saying so,
        rather than quietly spending something else.
    </p>

    <h2>The harness</h2>
    <p>
        The harness is the <strong>agentic loop</strong> that serves the turn, and it is a different axis from the provider: the same model
        behaves differently under each, which is why the picker asks separately. Each provider runs on the loops below, its default first;
        where there is more than one, the picker shows one chip per loop.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Loops</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                loops.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>{row.provider}</strong></td>
                        <td>
                            {row.loops}
                            {row.note === undefined ? null : <span class="text-subtle"> {row.note}</span>}
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        The picker remembers the loop per provider, so choosing Claude Code for Codex says nothing about Grok. A request naming a loop
        the provider doesn't offer runs the provider's own, and is recorded that way: the conversation, its transcript and the usage
        ledger name the loop that actually served the turn.
    </p>
    <p>
        The choice matters because the loops support different features, and the app tells you before you find out mid-turn. A runtime lacking mid-turn steering, clarifying questions, per-tool approvals, MCP tools or reasoning-effort control lists those limits, names another loop of the same provider that lifts them where there is one, and the composer hides the controls it would ignore. The composer's model pill names the loop whenever it isn't the provider's default.
    </p>

    <h2>What each vendor allows</h2>
    <p>
        Some routes work but sit outside what the vendor allows. They stay available, and the picker marks them quietly: a small
        icon beside the provider and on that loop's chip, and a <em>Vendor terms</em> row whose hover card says what the vendor allows.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Google</strong>: the
            <a href="https://antigravity.google/terms/">Antigravity terms</a> don't allow its sign-in in third-party clients such as
            OpenCode, and Google has suspended accounts for it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Meta</strong>: a Muse Code subscription key is
            <a href="https://dev.meta.ai/docs/muse-code/subscriptions">for use with Muse Code only</a>. Muse Code is Meta's default loop
            here; choosing Claude Code instead runs the key outside those terms.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Codex and Grok under Claude Code</strong>: OpenAI and xAI
            <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001542">list</a> the
            <a href="https://x.ai/news/grok-opencode">apps</a> a plan may run in, and Claude Code, reached through the bundled
            translator, isn't one of them. Nothing on record forbids it.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Per-turn controls</h2>
    <p>
        Beyond the model there is reasoning effort, extended thinking, and a fast-speed request: each offered only where the runtime
        actually honours it. A conversation remembers what its last turn ran with, so opening it again seeds the composer from{" "}
        <em>that</em> conversation rather than from whatever another tab last picked.
    </p>
    <p>
        Permission modes are the other half. A runtime that reports the full axis gets per-tool approval cards; one that does not gets propose-then-approve or run, nothing between. There the container is the isolation boundary and every tool call inside it is pre-approved.
    </p>

    <h2>Models for work you didn't start</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>Sandbox → Agent → Models</strong> holds one ordered list <em>per job</em>: commit messages, session titles, the safety
        judge, loop verdicts, pipeline fixes, deployment fixes, maintenance chores, documentation runs, acceptance runs, automation
        wakes and the rest. Each list is tried top to bottom, so one spent account doesn't take a job down, and each entry
        carries how its model runs — effort, thinking, speed, harness — as well as which model it is.
    </p>
    <p>A row you haven't touched falls back to its own default, and the two defaults are genuinely different:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>One-shot helpers</strong> — a commit message, a session title, a verdict — default to <strong>Auto</strong>: the
            cheapest rung of every connected provider, best first, resolved at the moment it's read. So it can never name a provider you
            have no credential for, and it improves by itself when you connect one. Nothing stops you pinning a frontier model to your
            commit subjects instead; that's a preference, and it's yours to have. A model kept for one-shot jobs, one that cannot call
            tools or the small local one, can be pinned here and nowhere else.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Whole sessions</strong> — an automation's wake, a chore, an acceptance run, the fix a refused push proposes —
            fall back to whatever your own chat is set to. Nothing can judge what a session is worth, and a wrong guess is billed in whole
            sessions, so the honest default is a model you already chose.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    {/* Distinguish provider usage from intentic's free service before describing costs. */}
    <p>
        Two different kinds of money, and only one of them is a bill. <strong>intentic itself is free</strong>: every sandbox, every
        capability and team sharing, with no tiers and no card. What a turn costs is <em>your</em> provider subscription being spent, because
        the credential is yours and intentic never resells inference. The one optional charge anywhere is a hosted sandbox, described on{" "}
        <a href="/pricing/">Pricing</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>Sandbox → Usage</strong> reports the durable spend ledger: a row appended when a turn ends, grouped by day, provider, account and model, with cache hit rates alongside. Rows are written daemon-side and never edited, so it records what was spent rather than estimating it. Agent cards carry their own cost, and a workflow step can be capped in dollars: the one resource you cannot recover after an unattended fan-out.
    </p>
    <p>
        When a provider refuses a turn because an allowance is spent or a plan is off, the last refusal is kept and shown beside the
        account, so a fleet that has quietly stopped working says why.
    </p>

    <DocsRelated
        links={[
            { href: "/docs/automations/", title: "Automations & workflows", why: "where pinning an account and a model actually pays off." },
            { href: "/docs/parallel-agents/#full-child-agents-the-meta-harness", title: "Full child agents", why: "children that choose a provider and harness independently." },
            { href: "/docs/remote-runners/#credentials-and-capabilities", title: "Remote runners", why: "which model credentials can serve a turn on another machine." },
            { href: "/docs/access/", title: "Access & sharing", why: "who else may spend this sandbox's allowance." },
        ]}
    />
</DocsLayout>
